{"id":1436,"date":"2020-11-27T16:57:45","date_gmt":"2020-11-28T00:57:45","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=1436"},"modified":"2023-08-21T08:40:36","modified_gmt":"2023-08-21T15:40:36","slug":"upload-your-own-code","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/upload-your-own-code\/","title":{"rendered":"How to upload code"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215e7bd670\" name=\"6a2215e7bd670\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Connect your cable<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]The first step you need to do is connect your uploading cable. There are three parts:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Micro USB cable<\/li>\n<li>UART cable<\/li>\n<li>USB to UART adapter<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_images_carousel images=&#8221;1383,1382,1381&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221;][vc_column_text]Connect the adapter to one end of the UART cable. Then, connect the Micro USB end to the adapter.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;1415&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215e7bd8ce\" name=\"6a2215e7bd8ce\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Programming mode<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]First, set your Smart Inventor Board to programming mode:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Flip DIP switch 1 up to the &#8220;ON&#8221; position<\/li>\n<li>Connect the UART cable end of the download cable in the port on the lower right side of the Smart Inventor board.<\/li>\n<li>Connect the USB end to your computer. If you have USB-C ports, make sure you have an adapter nearby.<\/li>\n<li>Your blue LED should be flashing. If it&#8217;s not, tap the reset button below the DIP switches. <strong>Do not hold this button down because it will reset the board.<\/strong><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;1437&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215e7bd965\" name=\"6a2215e7bd965\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Open your sketch<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Next, open the\u00a0<em>Arduino IDE<\/em> and open the sketch (or code) that you want to upload to the board.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;3011&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215e7bd9c4\" name=\"6a2215e7bd9c4\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Select the correct board<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Once you decide on your sketch, make sure you select the correct board in Arduino. Click on Tools &gt; Board and then scroll all the way down to find your board.<\/p>\n<p>Not sure which board is yours? On the front of your Smart Inventor Board, you should have an area near the center that says &#8220;Smart Inventor&#8221; with the version number, and that should match one of your options in Arduino. For example, if you have Smart Inventor V2, you should choose Rokit-SmartInventor-mega32_v2.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;1438&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215e7bda32\" name=\"6a2215e7bda32\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Select the correct serial port<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Find the serial port connected to your Smart Inventor Board:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>For Mac look for &#8220;\/dev\/cu.SLAB_USBtoUART&#8221; or &#8220;\/dev\/cu.usbserial&#8221;<\/li>\n<li>For Windows, look for a COM port number that is\u00a0<strong>not<\/strong> COM1<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;1439&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_single_image image=&#8221;1440&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215e7bda81\" name=\"6a2215e7bda81\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 6<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Upload the sketch<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]You&#8217;re almost done! Upload the sketch by clicking on the upload icon, which is an arrow pointing to the right. Once the file completely uploads to your Smart Inventor Board, Arduino IDE will display \u201cDone uploading\u201d in the bottom left corner. If you have any errors, make sure to resolve those code issues first.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;1441&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_single_image image=&#8221;1442&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215e7bdac6\" name=\"6a2215e7bdac6\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 7<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Initialize the program!<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Finally, flip DIP switch 1 down to the &#8220;OFF&#8221; position to initialize your downloaded program. Done![\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;1443&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 8 Connect your cable [vc_column_text]The first step you need to do is connect your uploading cable. There are three parts: Micro USB cable UART cable USB to UART&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/upload-your-own-code\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":1734,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":false,"_lesson_complexity":"","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":802,"_lesson_preview":"preview"},"lesson-category":[1753,1777,1793,1617,423,626,1533,1438,853,59,171],"lesson-tag":[2163,2177,2122,2171],"class_list":["post-1436","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-arduino","lesson-tag-getting-started","lesson-tag-programming","lesson-tag-rokit-smart","module-getting-started","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/1436","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/lesson"}],"version-history":[{"count":4,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/1436\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":6615,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/1436\/revisions\/6615"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media\/1734"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media?parent=1436"}],"wp:term":[{"taxonomy":"lesson-category","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-category?post=1436"},{"taxonomy":"lesson-tag","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-tag?post=1436"}],"curies":[{"name":"wp","href":"https:\/\/api.w.o